"Agentic In-Context Learning: What Algorithms Can Transformers Learn in Closed-Loop Interaction? (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23051v1)\nciteturn41231view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23051v1\",\"lineno\":275}); Total lines: 1027\nL244: The confidence weight $\\alpha$ trades top-answer probability against normalized certainty, $\\tau_{v}$ controls how strongly each observation updates the verifier belief, and the credit discount $\\gamma$ controls how much later verifier progress is assigned to the current action. The primary setting uses $(\\alpha,\\tau_{v},\\gamma)=(.75,.75,0)$, and we refer to its $\\gamma=0$ objective as the one-step process reward.\nL245: Following Proposition cite125†5.1 , the outcome-preserving process reward uses a perturbed potential-shaped dense signal while preserving the outcome-optimal policy set.\nL246: To describe the learned policy with (cite129†7 ), here we consider four candidates sharing the Bayesian update but using different decision rules: Bayes DP, one-step knowledge gradient (KG), one-step entropy reduction, and an open-loop oracle. These candidates distinguish outcome-optimal planning, one-step value of information, a surrogate information objective, and feedback-insensitive acquisition.\nL247: Appendix cite58†G gives the formal definitions of the rewards and candidate policies, the diagnostic histories, and the complete training protocol.\nL248: Figure 3: Distances to candidate policies under four training objectives. Each panel reports policy TV to the four candidates on a shared diagnostic set.\nL249: Figure cite130†3 shows that outcome reward training produces behavior closest to Bayes DP, whereas one-step process reward training produces behavior closest to KG. The latter is consistent with the one-step objective violating the ordering condition in Proposition cite125†5.1 . The outcome-preserving process reward instead remains Bayes DP-like. The bounded perturbation changes individual step rewards, but telescoping leaves the ordering of cumulative action values unchanged.\nL250: This provides an empirical test of the proposition’s ordering criterion without requiring the process and outcome rewards to be numerically equal. Appendix Table cite131†8 reports the full candidate comparison. We also perform a 12-cell sweep over $(\\alpha,\\tau_{v})$ (Appendix cite68†G.6 ). The primary $(.75,.75)$ result is not an isolated parameter setting: the nearest candidate changes with the verifier reward specification, while the primary KG-like behavior persists across a neighborhood of tested settings.\nL251: All tested one-step reward settings with $\\gamma=0$ exhibit ordering violations on the diagnostic set and are closest to candidates other than Bayes DP.\nL252: #### Verifier calibration and credit assignment.\nL253: We next isolate credit assignment $\\gamma$ while holding the local score $(\\alpha,\\tau_{v})$ fixed. We vary $\\gamma$ to test when the objective satisfies the ordering condition in Proposition cite125†5.1 . We fix $\\alpha=\\tau_{v}=1$ because at $\\gamma=0$, its optimal-action set coincides with that of one-step KG; at $\\gamma=1$, its expected cumulative return equals the expected outcome return up to an action-independent baseline, so its optimal-action set coincides with that of Bayes DP.\nL254: Now the ordering condition in Proposition cite125†5.1 depends on $\\gamma$ and on the histories being evaluated. We prespecify a margin-separated set of diagnostic histories that distinguish Bayes DP from KG and use it to evaluate how ordering preservation and learned behavior change with $\\gamma$.\nL255: Figure 4: Varying the credit discount $\\gamma$ at $\\alpha=\\tau_{v}=1$. The gray band marks the common ordering/decision crossover; the green band marks zero oracle violations, after which learned agreement is nearly constant.\nL256: Figure cite132†4 tests Proposition cite125†5.1 ’s ordering criterion empirically. Panel (a) reports the fractions of diagnostic histories satisfying or violating $\\mathcal{A}_{S,t}^{*}(h)\\subseteq\\mathcal{A}_{\\mathrm{out},t}^{*}(h)$, and panel (b) reports the learned policies’ top-action agreement with Bayes DP and KG. Both pairs of curves cross between $\\gamma=.50$ and $.70$.\nL257: On this diagnostic set, the proposition’s strict condition is reached between $.90$ and $.95$, when the violation rate becomes zero; thereafter learned agreement and terminal return remain nearly constant. Thus extending credit moves the objective from KG-like local ordering to Bayes DP-consistent ordering, and the trained policies follow the same transition.\nL258: Appendix cite70†G.8 describes how we construct the diagnostic sets and reports agreement with each objective’s optimal actions, minimum action-value gaps, and comparisons with additional candidates.\nL259: Returning to the state–update–decision decomposition, posterior decoding and update-identity checks yield similar errors across objectives despite their different decisions. These supporting diagnostics and controlled decision tests are reported in Appendix cite61†G.3 .\nL260: ### 6.4 Natural-language tool use with a pretrained LLM\nL261: The symbolic experiments show that learned decisions change with the training objective. We now test whether these differences persist when a pretrained language model receives the same task information through natural-language descriptions. We fine-tune Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct (cite133†Qwen et al., 2025 ) on BTS with outcome reward, one-step process reward, and Bayes DP-score distillation, then evaluate training templates, unseen paraphrases, and a shuffled-description control.\nL262: Appendix cite76†G.9 gives the protocol.\nL263: Figure 5: Natural-language tool use under three training objectives. Top: paired bars compare distances to Bayes DP and KG references; the closer candidate is unchanged under unseen paraphrases. Bottom: task returns decrease modestly under paraphrases and sharply under shuffled cards, and move all policies far from both references. Appendix Table cite134†24 reports all values.\nL264: Figure cite135†5 shows that these behavioral differences persist with language inputs and unseen paraphrases. Outcome reward training is Bayes DP-like, whereas one-step process reward training is KG-like. Distillation most closely matches its supplied Bayes DP reference. Appendix Table cite136†25 compares the models with additional candidates. The same nearest-candidate relationships persist across unseen paraphrases with modest return degradation.\nL265: Shuffling tool cards instead collapses returns and moves every policy far from both candidates. Though the model receives numerical observation models and a fixed action vocabulary that departures from real agentic task, generalization across task and tool paraphrases, together with the shuffled-description control, indicates dependence on description semantics rather than a fixed token-level rule.\nL266: ## 7 Conclusion and Limitations\nL267: We formalize Agentic ICL as interactive adaptation in which a fixed-parameter Transformer shapes its future evidence through actions, without requiring observations to reveal rewards. Our construction realizes regular finite-horizon recursions with a bound on autonomous return loss, and distillation demonstrates learned execution.\nL268: Reward experiments link decision differences to outcome-optimal action ordering: a perturbed process reward that preserves this ordering retains behavior close to outcome training, while objective interventions shift learned decisions as alignment changes. The outcome/process decision contrast also persists across textual paraphrases in a pretrained language model.\nL269: The theory establishes expressivity under regularity assumptions without guaranteeing optimization convergence. Behavioral comparisons are candidate-relative, and probes do not identify a unique internal algorithm. Experiments use small structured tasks, hand-specified verifiers, and a potential-based control whose perturbation preserves optimal actions exactly. These results do not establish robustness to arbitrary process-reward errors or learned verifiers.\nL270: Language-model tests supply observation models and constrain actions, leaving generalization to open-ended agents with unknown tool semantics, longer interactions, and context compression unresolved.\nL271: ## 8 AI Use Statement\nL272: In this work, we used generative AI tools to assist with proof writing and method implementation, obtain feedback on research methodology and experiments, propose or refine hypotheses, and generate synthetic datasets. We did not use these tools to develop theoretical models or conceptual frameworks, formulate mathematical claims, provide critical ingredients for mathematical proofs, translate text, conduct qualitative or thematic data analysis, or interpret results.\nL273: Dataset cleaning and reformatting are not applicable to this work. We reviewed all AI-assisted work. We checked LLM-generated proofs for errors and tested generated code for correctness. We take responsibility for all final text, claims, and artifacts, including AI-assisted content.\nL274: ## References\nL275:   * Akyürek et al. (2023) E. Akyürek, D. Schuurmans, J. Andreas, T. Ma, and D. Zhou What learning algorithm is in-context learning? investigations with linear models. In International Conference on Learning Representations, Cited by: cite137†§1 , cite138†§2 .\nL276:   * Auer et al. (2002a) P. Auer, N. Cesa-Bianchi, and P. Fischer Finite-time analysis of the multiarmed bandit problem. Machine Learning. Cited by: cite139†Appendix A , cite140†§2 .\nL277:   * Auer et al. (2002b) P. Auer, N. Cesa-Bianchi, Y. Freund, and R. E. Schapire The nonstochastic multiarmed bandit problem. SIAM Journal on Computing. Cited by: cite141†Appendix A , 