Machine Learning, Cited by: cite149†§2 .\nL312:   * Yao et al. (2023) S. Yao, J. Zhao, D. Yu, N. Du, I. Shafran, K. Narasimhan, and Y. Cao ReAct: synergizing reasoning and acting in language models. In International Conference on Learning Representations, Cited by: cite153†§1 , cite149†§2 .\nL313:   * Yun et al. (2020) C. Yun, S. Bhojanapalli, A. S. Rawat, S. J. Reddi, and S. Kumar Are transformers universal approximators of sequence-to-sequence functions?. In International Conference on Learning Representations, Cited by: cite146†§2 .\nL314:   * Zanette and Brunskill (2018) A. Zanette and E. Brunskill Problem dependent reinforcement learning bounds which can identify bandit structure in MDPs. In International Conference on Machine Learning, Cited by: cite140†§2 .\nL315:   * Zhao et al. (2024) A. Zhao, D. Huang, Q. Xu, M. Lin, Y. Liu, and G. Huang ExpeL: LLM agents are experiential learners. In AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence, Cited by: cite153†§1 , cite149†§2 .\nL316: ## Appendix A Instances of the Agentic ICL Recursion\nL317: \nL318: The examples below instantiate (cite121†2 ) for sequential information acquisition, bandits, and online RL. Sequential information acquisition includes active learning and Bayesian experimental design, where queries may return measurements used for a terminal decision (cite160†Chaloner and Verdinelli, 1995 ). Table cite161†2 summarizes the interfaces; the equations below specify their updates and decision rules.\nL319: Let $\\delta_{a}$ denote a point mass, $\\mathsf{B}(b;a,x)$ the action-conditioned Bayes update, and $\\operatorname{SoftArgMax}_{\\beta}\\{q(a)\\}$ the distribution proportional to $\\exp(\\beta q(a))$ over legal actions. All argmax rules use a fixed measurable tie convention.\nL320: #### Regularity qualifications for Table cite122†1 .\nL321: The listed instances inherit the sufficient conditions of Theorem cite123†4.1 : a fixed finite horizon, compact fixed-width encodings, a finite global action vocabulary, and a public-history legal-action mask. Decision kernels must have continuous finite logits; deterministic argmax rules are therefore softened, or the approximation is restricted to positive-margin subdomains.\nL322: Bayesian likelihoods are bounded below by $p_{\\min}>0$, and empirical quotients use positive pseudocounts so their updates remain regular; empirical-model states are pre-normalized. These conditions are sufficient for the compiler, not necessary conditions for Transformer implementation.\nL323: Time-varying spaces can be embedded by padding into fixed ambient spaces.\nL324: Table 2: Observations, actions, environment kernels, and learner states for each algorithm, grouped by interaction setting. $N_{t},S_{t}$ are bandit statistics; $N_{t},C_{t},Z_{t}$ in the model-based rows are transition counts, reward sums, and visit counts. Terminal flags $d_{t}$ make bootstrapping conventions explicit.\nL325: Algorithmic instance  | Returned record $x_{t}\\in\\mathcal{X}_{t}$  | Action $a_{t}\\in\\mathcal{A}_{t}$  | Environment kernel $K_{M}$  | Information state $s_{t}\\in\\mathcal{S}_{t}$\nL326: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL327: Setting: sequential information acquisition\nL328: Pool-based active learning (BALD)  | $(z_{t},y_{t})\\in\\mathcal{Z}\\times\\mathcal{Y}$  | $\\operatorname{query}(z_{t})$, $z_{t}\\in\\mathcal{P}_{t}$  | $p_{M}(y_{t}\\mid z_{t})$, with the queried $z_{t}$ copied into the record  | $(\\mathcal{D}_{t},\\mathcal{P}_{t},b_{t})$: labeled set, unlabeled pool, posterior\nL329: Bayesian sequential design (VOI/KG)  | $x_{t}\\in\\mathcal{X}_{t}(a_{t})$  | query or experiment  | $K_{M}(x\\mid h_{t-1},a_{t})$  | $(h_{t-1},b_{t})$, $b_{t}(m)=\\mathbb{P}(M=m\\mid h_{t-1})$\nL330: Setting: bandits\nL331: Thompson sampling  | reward $r_{t}\\in[0,1]$  | arm $i_{t}\\in[K]$  | $r_{t}\\sim P_{M,i_{t}}$  | posterior $b_{t}(M)$ over arm-reward models\nL332: UCB  | reward $r_{t}\\in[0,1]$  | arm $i_{t}\\in[K]$  | $r_{t}\\sim P_{M,i_{t}}$  | $(t,N_{t},S_{t})$, with counts and reward sums in $\\mathbb{R}^{K}$\nL333: Linear contextual bandit  | $(r_{t},c_{t+1})$  | arm $i_{t}\\in[K]$ after seeing $c_{t}$  | $r_{t}=\\phi(c_{t},i_{t})^{\\top}\\theta_{*}+\\varepsilon_{t}$, then $c_{t+1}\\sim\\nu$  | $(c_{t},V_{t},q_{t})$, ridge matrix and response vector\nL334: EXP3  | selected-arm reward $r_{t}(i_{t})\\in[0,1]$  | arm $i_{t}\\in[K]$  | arbitrary reward vector $r_{M,t}(h_{t-1})$, with only coordinate $i_{t}$ revealed  | $(t,w_{t})$, positive arm weights in $\\mathbb{R}^{K}$\nL335: Setting: online reinforcement learning\nL336: Q-learning  | $(r_{t},o_{t+1},d_{t})$  | off-policy control $a_{t}$  | controlled transition/reward law  | $(Q_{t},o_{t})$\nL337: SARSA  | $(r_{t},o_{t+1},d_{t})$  | on-policy control $a_{t}$  | controlled transition/reward law  | $(Q_{t},o_{t},p_{t})$, $p_{t}$ a pending transition or $\\bot$\nL338: Actor-critic  | $(r_{t},o_{t+1},d_{t})$  | $a_{t}\\sim\\pi_{\\theta_{t}}(\\cdot\\mid o_{t})$  | controlled transition/reward law  | $(\\theta_{t},w_{t},o_{t})$: actor, critic, current observation\nL339: Learned-model planning  | $(r_{t},o_{t+1},d_{t})$  | control action  | unknown controlled model $(P_{M},R_{M})$  | $(o_{t},N_{t},C_{t},Z_{t},\\widehat{P}_{t},\\widehat{R}_{t})$\nL340: UCBVI  | $(r_{k,j},o_{k,j+1},d_{k,j})$  | episodic control $a_{k,j}$  | unknown length-$L$ episodic MDP  | $(k,j,o_{k,j})$; empirical model statistics; optimistic $Q_{k}^{+},V_{k}^{+}$\nL341: #### Pool-based active learning.\nL342: \nL343: For a label model $p_{m}(y\\mid z)$, initialize $b_{1}$ by conditioning the prior on the seed labels $\\mathcal{D}_{1}$. Querying $z_{t}\\in\\mathcal{P}_{t}$ returns $y_{t}$ and gives\nL344: \nL345:  | $$b_{t+1}(m)\\propto b_{t}(m)p_{m}(y_{t}\\mid z_{t}),\\qquad U_{t}(s_{t},a_{t},x_{t})=(\\mathcal{D}_{t}\\cup\\{(z_{t},y_{t})\\},\\mathcal{P}_{t}\\setminus\\{z_{t}\\},b_{t+1}).$$  |  | (8)\nL346: BALD (cite162†Houlsby et al., 2011 ) selects queries using $G_{t}=\\operatorname{SoftArgMax}_{\\beta_{t}}\\{\\alpha_{t}(z)\\}_{z\\in\\mathcal{P}_{t}}$, where\nL347: \nL348:  | $$\\alpha_{t}(z)=\\mathsf{H}\\!\\left(\\sum_{m}b_{t}(m)p_{m}(\\cdot\\mid z)\\right)-\\sum_{m}b_{t}(m)\\mathsf{H}\\!\\left(p_{m}(\\cdot\\mid z)\\right).$$  |  | (9)\nL349: \nL350: A terminal prediction can be included as an answer action.\nL351: #### Bayesian sequential experimental design and value of information.\nL352: \nL353: At the pre-action history $h=h_{t-1}$, let $b_{t}(m)=\\mathbb{P}(M=m\\mid h)$. For a finite task family, the update and decision rule are\nL354:  | $\\displaystyle b_{t+1}(m)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{K_{m}(x_{t}\\mid h,a_{t})b_{t}(m)}{\\sum_{m^{\\prime}}K_{m^{\\prime}}(x_{t}\\mid h,a_{t})b_{t}(m^{\\prime})},$  |  | (10)\nL355:  | $\\displaystyle\\operatorname{supp}G_{t}(\\cdot\\mid h,b_{t})$  | $\\displaystyle\\subseteq\\arg\\max_{a}\\mathbb{E}_{m\\sim b_{t}}\\!\\left[r_{m}(h,a)+\\mathbb{E}_{x^{\\prime}\\sim K_{m}(\\cdot\\mid h,a)}V_{t+1}(\\mathsf{B}(b_{t};a,x^{\\prime}),(h,a,x^{\\prime}))\\right],$  |  | (11)\nL356: where $\\mathsf{B}$ is the update in (cite163†10 ); for pure acquisition, $r_{m}$ may be zero and utility may be terminal. This is a Bayesian sequential experimental-design instance: $a_{t}$ is a query or experiment and $x_{t}$ is its outcome. Sequential active learning is the label-query specialization given above. The continuation term captures the feedback loop: an experiment is valuable partly because it changes the posterior used to select later experiments or make a terminal decision.\nL357: A concrete one-step value-of-information rule is the knowledge gradient (cite111†Frazier et al., 2008 ). Let $\\mathcal{D}$ be a terminal decision set, $u(d,m)$ its utility under task $m$, and\nL358: \nL359:  | $$W(b):=\\max_{d\\in\\mathcal{D}}\\mathbb{E}_{m\\sim b}u(d,m).$$  |\nL360: \nL361: For the posterior-predictive law $\\overline{K}_{b}(x\\mid h,a)=\\sum_{m}b(m)K_{m}(x\\mid h,a)$, define\nL362:  | $\\displaystyle\\nu_{t}^{\\rm KG}(a\\mid h,b)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{x\\sim\\overline{K}_{b}(\\cdot\\mid h,a)}\\!\\left[W(\\mathsf{B}(b;a,x))-W(b)\\right],$  |  | (12)\nL363:  | $\\displaystyle G_{t}(a\\mid h,b)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{\\exp\\{\\beta_{t}[\\nu_{t}^{\\rm KG}(a\\mid h,b)-c_{t}(a)]\\}}{\\sum_{a^{\\prime}\\in\\mathcal{L}_{t}(h)}\\exp\\{\\beta_{t}[\\nu_{t}^{\\rm KG}(a^{\\prime}\\mid h,b)-c_{t}(a^{\\prime})]\\}},\\qquad a\\in\\mathcal{L}_{t}(h),$  |\nL364: where $c_{t}(a)$ is an optional experiment cost. The finite-horizon rule in (cite164†11 ) values all adaptive continuations, whereas (cite165†12 ) values the expected one-step improvement in terminal utility. Both use the same posterior state and Bayes update.\nL365: #### Thompson sampling.\nL366: \nL367: Initialize $b_{1}$ from $x_{0}$ and update by (cite163†10 ) using the selected arm’s reward law $P_{m,i_{t}}(r_{t})$. With $\\mu_{i}(m)=\\mathbb{E}_{P_{m,i}}r$, Thompson sampling (cite107†Russo et al., 2018 ) draws $\\widetilde{M}_{t}\\sim b_{t}$ and selects $\\operatorname{tieargmax}_{i}\\mu_{i}(\\widetilde{M}_{t})$. For Theorem cite123†4.1 , the full-support relaxation $(1-\\eta)G_{t}+\\eta\\operatorname{Unif}([K])$, $\\eta>0$, has finite logits given by its log probabilities.\nL368: #### UCB bandit.\nL369: \nL370: For a stochastic $K$-armed bandit with revealed reward $r_{t}$, let $s_{t}=(t,N_{t},S_{t})$, where $N_{t}(i)$ and $S_{t}(i)$ are the pre-decision pull count and reward sum, initialized to zero. The UCB recursion (cite105†Auer et al., 2002a ) is, for every arm $i$,\nL371:  | $\\displaystyle N_{t+1}(i)$  | $\\displaystyle=N_{t}(i)+\\mathbf{1}\\{i=i_{t}\\},$  |  | (13)\nL372:  | $\\displaystyle S_{t+1}(i)$  | $\\displaystyle=S_{t}(i)+r_{t}\\mathbf{1}\\{i=i_{t}\\},$  |\nL373:  | $\\displaystyle\\widehat{\\mu}_{t+1}(i)$  | $\\displaystyle=S_{t+1}(i)/N_{t+1}(i),$  |\nL374:  | $\\displaystyle i_{t+1}^{*}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\operatorname{tieargmax}_{i\\in[K]}\\left[\\widehat{\\mu}_{t+1}(i)+\\beta_{t+1}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(t+1)}{N_{t+1}(i)}}\\right],\\qquad G_{t+1}=\\delta_{i_{t+1}^{*}}.$  |\nL375: Set the index of an unpulled arm to $+\\infty$; use the quotient above only after the arm’s first pull. A softened UCB kernel replaces the final point mass by softmax over the displayed indices.\nL376: #### Linear contextual bandit.\nL377: \n"
